WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.918
Welcome to Module 9 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:03.918 --> 00:00:06.175
deploying to test and production.

3
00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:08.038
This module is optional.

4
00:00:08.038 --> 00:00:11.782
It's for whoever deploys the
kit, and it follows Chapter 8 of

5
00:00:11.782 --> 00:00:16.074
the developer workbook, the deployment
runbook, across every gate.

6
00:00:16.074 --> 00:00:18.287
One thing first, and it matters.

7
00:00:18.287 --> 00:00:23.713
Nobody has run this runbook against a
live tenant, account, tenancy or project.

8
00:00:23.713 --> 00:00:27.635
Its deploy scripts have been run
only in fixture mode, against a

9
00:00:27.635 --> 00:00:32.312
fictional test and production environment,
and that dry-run output is real.

10
00:00:32.312 --> 00:00:35.624
Every live result in the
chapter is labeled expected.

11
00:00:35.624 --> 00:00:38.925
This module's Lab 1 runs only the dry run.

12
00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:41.075
It takes about fifty-five minutes.

13
00:00:41.075 --> 00:00:43.876
You'll dry-run the
pre-flight, the weekly run,

14
00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:47.600
the stage check and the
rollback plans on fixtures.

15
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:52.400
Why a runbook?

16
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.313
Each provider's Lab 2 proves
one provider in isolation.

17
00:00:56.313 --> 00:00:59.258
A deployment proves them
together, on a schedule,

18
00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.688
with a rollback someone has already run.

19
00:01:01.688 --> 00:01:06.681
The runbook is one set of commands,
run twice: once against test tenants,

20
00:01:06.681 --> 00:01:11.675
accounts, tenancies and projects,
then unchanged against production.

21
00:01:11.675 --> 00:01:13.625
Only the settings file changes.

22
00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:18.175
The order puts each step where it can be
undone before the next depends on it.

23
00:01:18.175 --> 00:01:20.850
Reads come first, one provider at a time.

24
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.200
Routing runs as a dry run
before anything is sent.

25
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.738
The one write to a cloud, the
Service Health alert policy,

26
00:01:27.738 --> 00:01:29.737
comes only after the reads work.

27
00:01:29.737 --> 00:01:31.538
And the schedule comes last.

28
00:01:31.538 --> 00:01:34.235
In production, routing stays a dry run for

29
00:01:34.235 --> 00:01:37.012
the first week before
sending is turned on.

30
00:01:37.012 --> 00:01:38.824
The order is design guidance.

31
00:01:38.824 --> 00:01:42.491
And remember the status: the
scripts ran only in fixture mode,

32
00:01:42.491 --> 00:01:45.864
and every live result is what
the code and the providers'

33
00:01:45.864 --> 00:01:49.510
pages say should happen, not
something anyone has seen.

34
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.173
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

35
00:01:56.173 --> 00:02:00.812
You'll describe the settings file's
rules: no secrets, no placeholder in

36
00:02:00.812 --> 00:02:05.372
an enabled provider, the stage it says
it is, and named notice contacts.

37
00:02:05.372 --> 00:02:09.947
You'll run the pre-flight and the weekly
run on fixtures, and read their checks.

38
00:02:09.947 --> 00:02:14.906
You'll show that the test stage's evidence
passes once a pre-flight and a weekly run

39
00:02:14.906 --> 00:02:18.311
are recorded, and that
production's pre-flight checks it,

40
00:02:18.311 --> 00:02:20.161
in the check called P F oh seven.

41
00:02:20.161 --> 00:02:22.806
You'll read the rollback
plan in reverse order,

42
00:02:22.806 --> 00:02:25.660
and show that an unapproved
hash applies nothing.

43
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.292
And you'll explain the promotion go/no-go,

44
00:02:28.292 --> 00:02:31.960
and why production routes as
a dry run for its first week.

45
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.072
You need Modules 1 to 8,
or at least their Lab 1s,

46
00:02:35.072 --> 00:02:39.110
because this module doesn't
teach the kit's chapters again.

47
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.587
Module 8 matters most,

48
00:02:40.587 --> 00:02:45.130
for the evidence chain and the
stage check this module passes.

49
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.068
Unit 1 is what the runbook adds.

50
00:02:51.068 --> 00:02:54.927
One settings file per stage holds
every value the commands need,

51
00:02:54.927 --> 00:02:59.005
and the settings reader enforces
its rules before any command runs.

52
00:02:59.005 --> 00:03:03.823
No secrets: a secret-like name, such
as password, client secret or token,

53
00:03:03.823 --> 00:03:07.374
with a value, stops the run,
and the ServiceNow token

54
00:03:07.374 --> 00:03:10.755
is read at run time from
an environment variable.

55
00:03:10.755 --> 00:03:13.393
No placeholders in an enabled provider.

56
00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:16.454
The stage it says it is,
checked by the pre-flight.

57
00:03:16.454 --> 00:03:19.792
And named notice contacts
for each enabled provider.

58
00:03:19.792 --> 00:03:21.805
Every collector call is a read.

59
00:03:21.805 --> 00:03:24.771
The kit's writes are
few, and each is gated:

60
00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:29.715
the Azure Policy assignment and
its remediation, and the rollback,

61
00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:33.400
run only with apply and the
plan's approved SHA-256;

62
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.793
payloads go to the agency's endpoint
only with send and the settings flag.

63
00:03:38.793 --> 00:03:42.129
The settings file also
names each provider's cloud.

64
00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:47.783
The estate the runbook writes for is
Microsoft 365 G C C with commercial Azure,

65
00:03:47.783 --> 00:03:51.493
on the worldwide Graph and
Resource Manager hosts.

66
00:03:51.493 --> 00:03:55.543
GCC High and DoD are other
names in the same file.

67
00:03:55.543 --> 00:04:00.030
The kit doesn't build the ServiceNow
endpoint; that's the agency's.

68
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.267
Unit 2 is the stages, and every
stage starts with the pre-flight.

69
00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:11.967
It reads only, and it runs
after the readers sign in.

70
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:16.538
P F oh one reads the settings and routing
files, and fails on the wrong stage,

71
00:04:16.538 --> 00:04:19.685
no provider enabled, routing
that doesn't validate,

72
00:04:19.685 --> 00:04:23.431
or sending turned on without an
endpoint and token variable.

73
00:04:23.431 --> 00:04:25.855
Then four checks per enabled provider.

74
00:04:25.855 --> 00:04:28.855
Oh two: the provider's tool is installed.

75
00:04:28.855 --> 00:04:32.693
Oh three: the signed-in cloud
is the one the settings name,

76
00:04:32.693 --> 00:04:35.780
not another cloud, tenant,
partition or realm.

77
00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.667
Oh four: the reader's first call works,
and for AWS that includes the support plan;

78
00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:45.780
without a qualifying plan the call fails
with a subscription required exception.

79
00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.742
Oh five: somebody is named to
receive the provider's notices.

80
00:04:49.742 --> 00:04:52.431
That's the pre-flight's link to S R eight.

81
00:04:52.431 --> 00:04:55.830
P F oh six checks that the
output root can be written.

82
00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.448
And P F oh seven runs in production only:

83
00:04:58.448 --> 00:05:02.867
it fails if the test stage's evidence
wouldn't pass its stage check.

84
00:05:02.867 --> 00:05:05.930
Production can't start
until test has passed.

85
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.992
Stage 1, the test stage, has nine steps.

86
00:05:12.992 --> 00:05:16.054
Sign in each reader,
then run the pre-flight.

87
00:05:16.054 --> 00:05:19.883
Then the staged enable: read each
enabled provider on its own,

88
00:05:19.883 --> 00:05:23.864
cross-check it against the provider's
own command, and record it,

89
00:05:23.864 --> 00:05:28.993
before the providers run together, because
a failure in one would hide the others.

90
00:05:28.993 --> 00:05:33.810
Route the full collection as a dry run, and
have the service desk read the payloads:

91
00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:37.189
every security advisory and
retirement should have one,

92
00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:40.568
with an assignment group the
change manager recognizes.

93
00:05:40.568 --> 00:05:45.156
Run the smoke test, four checks with
their expected results written down first.

94
00:05:45.156 --> 00:05:46.806
Then the one write to a cloud:

95
00:05:46.806 --> 00:05:51.902
the Service Health alert policy from
Microsoft Learn's guidance on deploying alerts

96
00:05:51.902 --> 00:05:54.989
at scale with Azure Policy,
at the test subscription,

97
00:05:54.989 --> 00:05:59.080
applied only with the hash a reviewer
who isn't the operator approved.

98
00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:03.239
Schedule the weekly run with Windows
Task Scheduler's own cmdlets,

99
00:06:03.239 --> 00:06:05.130
and check the stage's evidence.

100
00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.305
Finally, rehearse the rollback.

101
00:06:07.305 --> 00:06:11.061
Deleting the policy assignment
doesn't remove the resources

102
00:06:11.061 --> 00:06:15.110
the remediation created; the
change record decides on those.

103
00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:24.353
Promotion to production happens only
when all seven go/no-go criteria pass,

104
00:06:24.353 --> 00:06:28.960
and the ISSO and the change
advisory board record the decision.

105
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.085
The test stage's evidence passes.

106
00:06:31.085 --> 00:06:34.748
Every provider's collection
matched the provider's own command.

107
00:06:34.748 --> 00:06:36.286
The smoke test passed.

108
00:06:36.286 --> 00:06:37.785
The routing was reviewed.

109
00:06:37.785 --> 00:06:41.573
The alert policy applied, and
its rollback was rehearsed.

110
00:06:41.573 --> 00:06:43.909
Each provider's notice contacts are named.

111
00:06:43.909 --> 00:06:46.843
And the known gaps are
recorded with the ISSO.

112
00:06:46.843 --> 00:06:51.123
Stage 2 runs the same commands
with the production settings file.

113
00:06:51.123 --> 00:06:54.385
The pre-flight now includes P F oh seven.

114
00:06:54.385 --> 00:06:56.829
Providers are enabled one at a time again,

115
00:06:56.829 --> 00:07:00.135
and the alert policy is applied
at the management group.

116
00:07:00.135 --> 00:07:04.477
For the first week, routing stays a
dry run: the service desk works from

117
00:07:04.477 --> 00:07:09.123
the payloads by hand, so duplicate or
misrouted tasks don't reach the queue.

118
00:07:09.123 --> 00:07:12.734
Sending is turned on only after
the change manager approves.

119
00:07:12.734 --> 00:07:16.585
From then on, check the production
stage's evidence every week.

120
00:07:16.585 --> 00:07:20.033
It needs pre-flight, alerts,
run, route and digest,

121
00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:24.290
and a week with no new run
record means the run stopped.

122
00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:28.765
Try it.

123
00:07:28.765 --> 00:07:32.335
Lab 1 is the runbook's dry
run on the kit's fixtures,

124
00:07:32.335 --> 00:07:35.815
in the provider notices
folder, in PowerShell 7.

125
00:07:35.815 --> 00:07:39.160
The test and production
environments are fictional:

126
00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.440
nothing signs in, and nothing is sent.

127
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.390
Step one loads the deploy module
and checks the settings template.

128
00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:49.515
It's refused, because it still has
a placeholder in the Azure provider.

129
00:07:49.515 --> 00:07:52.115
A filled-in file prints its stage instead.

130
00:07:52.115 --> 00:07:54.990
Step two runs the test stage's pre-flight.

131
00:07:54.990 --> 00:08:00.776
The test fixture enables Azure, Microsoft
365 and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

132
00:08:00.776 --> 00:08:03.494
so there are fourteen checks: P F oh one,

133
00:08:03.494 --> 00:08:07.965
the four per-provider checks for
each of the three, and P F oh six.

134
00:08:07.965 --> 00:08:09.390
All fourteen pass.

135
00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:14.465
Step three records the pre-flight as
evidence, with the example change record.

136
00:08:14.465 --> 00:08:18.390
Its controls include S R
eight, for the named contacts.

137
00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.824
Step four runs the weekly
run on the day-one fixtures:

138
00:08:21.824 --> 00:08:26.536
fifteen notices from three providers,
eight creates routed as a dry run,

139
00:08:26.536 --> 00:08:30.640
and a digest with thirteen open
and five that need action.

140
00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.102
Step five checks the
test stage's evidence.

141
00:08:37.102 --> 00:08:42.478
Thirty-one files match the checksum file,
and five records form an unbroken chain.

142
00:08:42.478 --> 00:08:48.315
And every step the test stage needs
passes: pre-flight, run, route and digest.

143
00:08:48.315 --> 00:08:51.037
The two rows Module 8's
lab failed now pass,

144
00:08:51.037 --> 00:08:54.640
because the pre-flight and
the weekly run are recorded.

145
00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.430
Step six reads the records' index: the
pre-flight, then the weekly run's collection,

146
00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:04.728
routing and digest, then the
scheduled run itself, each pass.

147
00:09:04.728 --> 00:09:07.428
Step seven plans the
test stage's rollback,

148
00:09:07.428 --> 00:09:12.214
one step that unregisters the weekly
task if it exists, and changes nothing.

149
00:09:12.214 --> 00:09:17.103
Step eight runs production's pre-flight,
and hands it the test stage's evidence.

150
00:09:17.103 --> 00:09:21.978
Production enables all five providers,
so there are twenty-three checks,

151
00:09:21.978 --> 00:09:25.716
ending with P F oh seven: the
test stage's evidence passes.

152
00:09:25.716 --> 00:09:27.990
Twenty-three checks, none failed.

153
00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.109
If the test stage hadn't passed,

154
00:09:30.109 --> 00:09:34.110
P F oh seven would fail, and
production couldn't start.

155
00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:43.527
Step nine plans the Service Health alerts
at the production management group:

156
00:09:43.527 --> 00:09:48.235
two steps, assign and remediate, each
would apply, and a plan SHA-256.

157
00:09:48.235 --> 00:09:50.810
It's the same plan as Module 2's.

158
00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:55.547
Step ten plans production's rollback,
in reverse order of the deployment.

159
00:09:55.547 --> 00:09:58.823
First, unregister the
weekly scheduled task.

160
00:09:58.823 --> 00:10:01.347
Second, delete the policy assignment.

161
00:10:01.347 --> 00:10:05.822
Third, a manual step: list the resource
group the remediation created,

162
00:10:05.822 --> 00:10:11.160
because the change record decides on what
the remediation created, not the script.

163
00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.247
Dry run: nothing changed.

164
00:10:13.247 --> 00:10:16.113
Then the second command
tries to apply that

165
00:10:16.113 --> 00:10:18.898
plan with a hash nobody
approved, all zeros.

166
00:10:18.898 --> 00:10:22.237
It's refused: the approved hash must match

167
00:10:22.237 --> 00:10:25.872
the plan's SHA-256 before
apply runs anything.

168
00:10:25.872 --> 00:10:28.910
That's the guard against a
plan changed after approval.

169
00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:32.958
Step eleven runs the deploy
scripts' tests: twenty-five pass,

170
00:10:32.958 --> 00:10:37.998
none fail and none are skipped, on Windows,
where one of them runs only there.

171
00:10:37.998 --> 00:10:40.580
Then delete the dry run's folders.

172
00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:48.167
Lab 2 is the runbook itself,
and nobody has run it yet.

173
00:10:48.167 --> 00:10:53.212
You need a test environment for each provider
the agency enables, never production,

174
00:10:53.212 --> 00:10:56.428
with each reader already
granted in its module's Lab 2,

175
00:10:56.428 --> 00:10:58.256
and an approved change record.

176
00:10:58.256 --> 00:11:02.920
You also need people: someone who can
create policy and role assignments for the

177
00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:07.161
alert policy, a reviewer who isn't the
operator to approve each plan's hash,

178
00:11:07.161 --> 00:11:09.705
and a Windows account
for the scheduled run.

179
00:11:09.705 --> 00:11:14.411
Then follow Stage 1: fill and check
the settings file, sign in each reader,

180
00:11:14.411 --> 00:11:19.356
run the pre-flight, enable the providers
one at a time with a cross-check each,

181
00:11:19.356 --> 00:11:24.381
route as a dry run, run the smoke test,
apply the alert policy with its approved

182
00:11:24.381 --> 00:11:29.805
hash, schedule the weekly run, check the
stage's evidence and rehearse the rollback.

183
00:11:29.805 --> 00:11:35.643
Stage 2, production, comes only when
every go/no-go criterion passes.

184
00:11:35.643 --> 00:11:39.830
To back it out, Step 1.9
applies the approved rollback.

185
00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.830
The state, runs and evidence are kept.

186
00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:47.967
The federal requirement.

187
00:11:47.967 --> 00:11:51.017
Every runbook step ends
with a requirements table,

188
00:11:51.017 --> 00:11:53.993
and the kit writes an
evidence record for each run.

189
00:11:53.993 --> 00:11:58.318
The change record and the settings
file's hash support C M three,

190
00:11:58.318 --> 00:12:00.730
part b, reviewing proposed changes.

191
00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:04.891
The pre-flight's results support
S A nine, part c, and S R eight,

192
00:12:04.891 --> 00:12:09.311
notification agreements: every
reader works in its own cloud,

193
00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:12.605
and each provider's notice
contacts are named.

194
00:12:12.605 --> 00:12:19.295
The weekly run's folder, its records
and the weekly digest support S A nine,

195
00:12:19.295 --> 00:12:22.417
S I five, C A seven and R A three, part f.

196
00:12:22.417 --> 00:12:25.274
The evidence folder, checked
with the stage check,

197
00:12:25.274 --> 00:12:29.593
supports continuous monitoring and
the protection of audit information.

198
00:12:29.593 --> 00:12:33.092
And the rollback plan,
its results and its record

199
00:12:33.092 --> 00:12:36.592
support C M three and C P
two, the contingency plan.

200
00:12:36.592 --> 00:12:41.230
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

201
00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:45.042
The agency lines its evidence up
with them, and doesn't report them.

202
00:12:45.042 --> 00:12:47.508
Every alignment is the program's reading,

203
00:12:47.508 --> 00:12:51.920
and the assessor and the authorizing
official decide each control.

204
00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:57.645
Check yourself with two questions.

205
00:12:57.645 --> 00:13:01.983
First: production's pre-flight
fails P F oh seven.

206
00:13:01.983 --> 00:13:03.082
What does that mean?

207
00:13:03.082 --> 00:13:04.308
The answer is b.

208
00:13:04.308 --> 00:13:09.258
The test stage's evidence doesn't pass its
stage check, so production can't start.

209
00:13:09.258 --> 00:13:11.468
P F oh seven runs in production only,

210
00:13:11.468 --> 00:13:16.119
and it's the guard against deploying
to production before test has passed.

211
00:13:16.119 --> 00:13:20.245
Second: why does production route
as a dry run for its first week?

212
00:13:20.245 --> 00:13:21.470
The answer is b.

213
00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:24.957
So duplicate or misrouted
tasks don't reach the queue.

214
00:13:24.957 --> 00:13:29.526
The service desk works from the payloads
by hand, and sending is turned on only

215
00:13:29.526 --> 00:13:34.094
after the change manager approves, with
the settings flag and the send switch.

216
00:13:34.094 --> 00:13:36.522
The module page has three more questions,

217
00:13:36.522 --> 00:13:39.380
each linked to the
section that answers it.

218
00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:43.955
To sum up.

219
00:13:43.955 --> 00:13:48.455
You saw the settings template refused,
passed the test stage's pre-flight,

220
00:13:48.455 --> 00:13:52.955
recorded it, ran the weekly run, and
passed the test stage's evidence check.

221
00:13:52.955 --> 00:13:57.489
You passed production's pre-flight with
P F oh seven, planned the alerts at the

222
00:13:57.489 --> 00:14:00.007
management group, planned both rollbacks,

223
00:14:00.007 --> 00:14:02.742
and watched an unapproved
hash apply nothing.

224
00:14:02.742 --> 00:14:08.334
The evidence an ISSO keeps for the
deployment is each stage's evidence folder,

225
00:14:08.334 --> 00:14:12.353
with the change record, the
settings file and its SHA-256,

226
00:14:12.353 --> 00:14:18.293
the approved plan hashes and the weekly
digests filed beside it, on agency storage.

227
00:14:18.293 --> 00:14:21.367
Before shipping, work through
the chapter's checklist.

228
00:14:21.367 --> 00:14:25.688
And remember that this runbook hasn't
